# LF endings are not a style preference here — they are a correctness
# requirement. A Windows checkout that ships CRLF gives every script a trailing
# carriage return on its shebang line, and the failure on macOS or Linux reads
# as `bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M`, which is a confusing way to learn
# that a hook was never going to run.
# The catch-all comes FIRST and is the load-bearing line. An extension list alone
# leaves every extensionless file — LICENSE, NOTICE, .gitignore, this file — outside
# the rule, which is the same one-half-of-a-pair gap the README describes: each
# listed pattern is correct, and the defect is the entry that was never written.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit anyway, so the intent survives someone narrowing the catch-all.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.py   text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.md   text eol=lf
*.yml  text eol=lf
